      {"q":"Which simile is used in the poem?","a":"Words are like summer birds.","marks":1},
      {"q":"What does 'gaudy' mean?","a":"Showy in a cheap way.","marks":1},
      {"q":"What does the poet mean by 'words reach the head but never touch the heart'?","a":"Empty words may be understood but do not move the feelings, unlike sincere ones.","marks":3},
      {"q":"Explain 'A little said, and truly said'.","a":"It means few honest words can bring deeper joy than a flood of insincere ones.","marks":3},
      {"q":"Which poetic devices are used in the poem?","a":"Simile (summer birds), metaphor (heart as a pilgrim), personification (words summoned to the test) and regular rhyme.","marks":3},
      {"q":"Explain the theme of 'Words' in about 100 words.","a":"The poem says that words alone cannot satisfy the heart. They come and go like summer birds. Many words may be as worthless as weeds, and showy talk is like plants that blossom but bear no fruit. A few true words can bring joy and comfort. The poet stresses sincerity over quantity.","marks":5},
      {"q":"Discuss how the poet uses nature imagery to comment on speech.","a":"Summer birds show words are fleeting. Weeds show worthlessness. Plants that bloom without fruit show showy but empty words. The imagery makes the abstract idea of insincere speech concrete and memorable. Nature contrasts real results with outward show.","marks":5},
      {"q":"A student apologises with a long speech but does not change behaviour. Another apologises with a few honest words and helps.\n(i) Whose words matter more?\n(ii) Which line supports this?\n(iii) What is the lesson?","a":"(i) The second student's.\n(ii) A little said, and truly said, can deeper joy impart.\n(iii) Sincerity is more important than many words.","marks":4,"type":"case"}
